From: u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com (INLA help)
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2020 07:12:21 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] Including categorical variables with interactions
Message-ID: <PikRxcg0fBs.Am_8b7zUAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <PikRxcg0fBs.ZQHzn93NAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <PikRxcg0fBs.ZQHzn93NAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/PikRxcg0fBs/m/Am_8b7zUAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I’m not convinced anything is wrong, but if you want to keep all interaction you easily get some confounding issues, which makes, marginally, effects to be about the same, as only certain contrasts will be likelihood identifiable.  So you might have to define some linear combinations to extract what you want.

An alternative, is to switch to a sum-to-zero representation, like

> x = as.factor(letters[1:3])
> y = 1:3
> r = inla(y ~ 1 + f(x, model="iid", constr=TRUE, hyper=list(prec=list(initial=0,fixed=T))), data=data.frame(y,x))

So you get the overall mean in the intercept, and then the effects of ‘x’, 3 levels, which sums to zero.

--
Håvard Rue
Helpdesk
he...@r-inla.org

